    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a> records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> around 2450 BCE in the dispute over the Gu'edena, one of the earliest organised battles known, won by a dense <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> of spearmen.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a>, the last ruler of the First Dynasty, made judicial, social and economic reforms that may well be the first legal code known to have existed.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> cylinders found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> contain the longest known text in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-language">Sumerian language</a>, from a reign marked by riches, power and the building of temples.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The dynasties of Lagash are missing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>; the one tablet that lists its rulers, The Rulers of Lagash, is generally read as a satirical parody of the King List, its thirty kings given improbable reigns.</p></aside>
    </section>

