    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ur-nanshe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ur-nanshe.jpg" alt="Ur-Nanshe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ur-nanshe">Ur-Nanshe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur-Nanshe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lagash lay north-west of the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, at modern Al-Hiba in Dhi Qar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The Lagash state took in three cities: Lagash itself; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>, modern Telloh, some 25 km to the north-west, the religious centre with the E-ninnu temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>; and Nina, Tell Zurghul, about 10 km away at the state's southern limit, the three joined by a canal. Though some Uruk-period sherds have been found, significant occupation began early in the third millennium BCE, in the Early Dynastic I period (c. 2900–2600 BCE); a drone survey has suggested that the city grew on four marsh islands, some of them gated, though the notion is contested. Its peak, with an area of about 500 hectares, came in Early Dynastic III (c. 2500–2334 BCE), the age of the First Dynasty of Lagash.</p><p>The dynasties of Lagash are absent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>. Little is known of the first two rulers: En-hegal, believed to be the first, appears on a tablet recording a purchase of land, and his status and date are disputed; Lugalshaengur is named as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a> of Lagash on a mace head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesalim-of-kish">Mesilim</a>, who called himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nanshe">Ur-Nanshe</a>, 'son of Gu-NI.DU', left many inscriptions, nearly all about building temples; one records a channel dug against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a>, and several say he had the ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> as tribute. He claimed to have beaten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> in battle. His son Akurgal ruled briefly before Akurgal's own son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/enmetena"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enmetena.jpg" alt="Enmetena" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/enmetena">Enmetena</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Entemena,_Iraq_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lagash was a state of canals, temples and estates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>'s reign saw temples and palaces repaired or raised at Lagash and elsewhere and canals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> dug, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> was a major trading partner, as it had been when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nanshe">Ur-Nanshe</a> took its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> as tribute. The long quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> was over land and water: the Gu'edena, a fertile plain between the two cities, and the boundary channel that Eannatum extended from the Nun-channel, leaving a strip of 215 nindan, about 1,290 metres, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>'s land under Umma's control as a no-man's land.</p><p>Some 1,800 administrative tablets survive from the reigns of the last three rulers of the First Dynasty, Enentarzi, Lugalanda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a>, most from the 'woman's quarter', the temple of the goddess Babu, which was under the control of the queen. Excavation has found the working side of the city: an Early Dynastic administrative area with sealings and tablets of Eannatum, Enannatum I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmetena">Entemena</a>, an area of kilns, streets and houses, and in 2022 a public eatery with ovens, a cooling system, benches and great numbers of bowls and beakers. A building at the Bagara of Ningirsu with ovens, storage vats and a tablet naming 'the brewery' and 'a brewer' may have been a brewery or a temple kitchen. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> the Lagash state of Lagash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> and Nigin was the largest and most prosperous province of the empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures.jpg" alt="Stele of the Vultures" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_Vultures_in_the_Louvre_Museum_(enhanced_composite).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Background: Kikuyu3 Elements: Eric Gaba (Sting) Composite: पाटलिपुत्र 10:52, 30 April 2020 (UTC), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wars of Lagash are among the best recorded of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>, son of Akurgal and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nanshe">Ur-Nanshe</a>, made the city a major power across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and to the east. His inscriptions have him defeat 'mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>', Urua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, Akshak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> at a place called Antasur and kill Zuzu, king of Akshak. Around 2450 BCE the border dispute with Umma over the Gu'edena came to battle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a> shows Eannatum, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>, dismount from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and lead his men on foot; with spears lowered the army of Lagash advanced in a dense <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and after a brief clash won, one of the earliest organised battles known.</p><p>His brother Enannatum I crushed Ur-Lumma of Umma, who had hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from foreign lands, diverted water into the boundary channels of Ningirsu and Nanshe and burned the chapels on the levee, pursuing him to the town of LUM-ma-girnunta. Later Lagash was on the losing side: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a> was defeated by Lugalzagesi, first as ruler of Umma and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, and about 2300 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> 'laid waste' the territory from Lagash to the sea. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> reconquered the rebellious cities with mass slaughter, recording for Ur and Lagash 8,049 killed, 5,460 captured and enslaved and 5,985 expelled and annihilated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> campaigned to the east against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> and Elam; his sixth year was named for the smiting of Anshan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gudea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gudea.jpg" alt="Gudea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gudea_of_Lagash_Girsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jastrow, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The rulers of Lagash bore the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a> and ruled in the name of the city god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesalim-of-kish">Mesilim</a>, 'king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>', had mediated a formal border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> in the time of Enakalle, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a> restored his monument and the boundary markers without crossing into Umma's plain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmetena">Entemena</a> increased the power of Lagash and was a contemporary of Lugalkinishedudu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>. The last ruler of the First Dynasty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a>, was known for judicial, social and economic reforms that may well be the first legal code known to have existed.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> Lugal-ushumgal and Puzur-Mama governed Lagash for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri">Shar-Kali-Sharri</a>; after Shar-Kali-Sharri's death Puzur-Mama declared the city independent, though the Akkadian ruler Dudu still reported taking booty there. The Second Dynasty, unlike the first, dated its documents by year names, most of them religious. Ur-Baba's daughters married three later rulers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a>, Nam-mahani and Ur-gar, and he appointed Enanepada high priestess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, an office Naram-Sin had filled before him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> would fill after. Under Gudea thousands of inscriptions and countless statues were made, and twenty of his year names are known. After Ur-Nammu's conquest an ensi was a governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> terms; the empire's second official, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>, resided in the province. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a>'s fifth year his year names stop at Lagash, a sign that Ur had lost the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-language"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumerian-language.jpg" alt="Sumerian language" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-language">Sumerian language</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumerian_26th_c_Adab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lagash left records of a kind few early Sumerian cities match. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a> is among the earliest depictions of an organised battle, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> cylinders found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> contain the longest known text in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-language">Sumerian language</a>. The First Dynasty, though left off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, exercised considerable influence in the region in the time before the dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, and the prologue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu">Code of Ur-Nammu</a> boasts 'He slew Nam-ha-ni the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a> of Lagash', a measure of how much the city still counted.</p><p>Its reach was felt in its neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>'s conquests ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and Urua in the east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, Akshak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> remained under Lagashite kings even as their power waned, until Lugalkinishedudu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> claimed it; after Akkad's fall Ur-Baba's son Enannipadda and Gudea's building works show Lagash again dominant at Ur. Some researchers now hold that Gudea's reign overlapped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>'s rather than falling well before it in a time of Gutian power, since Ur-Baba's appointment of a high priestess at Ur fell between those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> and Ur-Nammu. The site itself, roughly 3.5 by 1.5 km and one of the largest in the region, was first dug for six weeks by Robert Koldewey in 1887, who took it for an extended burial place; Thorkild Jacobsen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuad-safar">Fuad Safar</a> found the first evidence of its identification as Lagash in 1953, and excavation resumed in 2019 under Holly Pittman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ur-nammu.jpg" alt="Ur-Nammu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Ur-Nammu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Steve Harris, source, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lagash fell twice. The First Dynasty ended when Lugalzagesi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a>, and about 2300 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, laying waste the land from Lagash to the sea; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a>'s reconquest followed with its slaughter and enslavement. For several centuries Lagash was an Akkadian possession, governed by men such as Lugal-ushumgal. Independence under Puzur-Mama, Ur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> I and their successors was at first tenuous, and Lagash remained at best a small local power until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a>.</p><p>The Second Dynasty, often called the Gudean Dynasty, ended with Nam-mahani, known chiefly for his defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>, first ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> III empire. Nam-mahani's inscriptions were defaced and the statues of him and his wife beheaded, the heads never found, an act of damnatio memoriae attributed to Ur-Nammu. Under Ur the Lagash state was the largest and most prosperous province of the empire and the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>; after the fifth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> king, his year names were no longer used there, showing that Ur no longer controlled the city. Afterwards there was modest occupation in the Isin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and Old Babylonian periods, and then Lagash was largely deserted until a Seleucid fortress was built on the site in the 2nd century BCE. Much of the oldest city now lies under the water table. What survives is the record: the statues of Gudea, the cylinders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a> and the tablets by which the city's history is known.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Lagash, a Sumerian state of three cities on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> plain, reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a> around 2450 BCE, fell to Lugalzagesi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>, rose again under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a>, and ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> slew its last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a> and made it the richest province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> III empire.</em></p>

